53
00:03:28,370 --> 00:03:29,653
So as I mentioned,

54
00:03:29,653 --> 00:03:34,330
we're going to talk a little bit
about distributions and packages.

55
00:03:34,330 --> 00:03:38,120
When we say distribution with Linux,
what do we mean?

56
00:03:38,120 --> 00:03:41,706
We really mean just a collection
of a bunch of pre configured

57
00:03:41,706 --> 00:03:44,433
software that's maintained by community or

58
00:03:44,433 --> 00:03:48,550
in some cases by companies that
have created these distributions.

59
00:03:48,550 --> 00:03:53,382
So these are really just a collection of
software packages that either companies or

60
00:03:53,382 --> 00:03:56,833
community have found useful and
I think is a useful place for

61
00:03:56,833 --> 00:03:59,480
you to start when you're
setting up your pc.

62
00:03:59,480 --> 00:04:04,212
So you can think of it as like all of the
software applications that would go into,

63
00:04:04,212 --> 00:04:05,117
for instance,

64
00:04:05,117 --> 00:04:09,600
a default install of your laptop when
you bought it with Windows installed.

65
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:11,390
Same idea with the distribution.

66
00:04:11,390 --> 00:04:15,944
There's probably more packages
that are associated with, for

67
00:04:15,944 --> 00:04:19,589
instance, editing documents or
editing images.

68
00:04:19,589 --> 00:04:24,041
And each each distribution is going to be
slightly different on what they have by

69
00:04:24,041 --> 00:04:28,014
default, and each distribution is
going to be slightly different on

70
00:04:28,014 --> 00:04:30,570
how you add additional software packages.

71
00:04:30,570 --> 00:04:34,639
So the version in the configuration of the
software that's installed on the system

72
00:04:34,639 --> 00:04:37,540
is going to be managed by
what's called packages.

73
00:04:37,540 --> 00:04:41,650
Ubuntu is the distribution that we're
going to be using for this course.

74
00:04:41,650 --> 00:04:43,711
It's a pretty popular Linux distributions.

75
00:04:43,711 --> 00:04:47,081
So one of the nice things about it,
you can find a lot of help online and

76
00:04:47,081 --> 00:04:49,430
a lot of references to
people that are using it.

77
00:04:49,430 --> 00:04:52,684
Every distribution that you use is
going to have its own mechanism for

78
00:04:52,684 --> 00:04:55,230
adding software packages
through the command line.

79
00:04:55,230 --> 00:04:58,545
And so Ubuntu's is called
the Apt Package Manager,

80
00:04:58,545 --> 00:05:01,200
which we'll talk about in a minute here.

81
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:05,912
So one question you might have, especially
if you come from a Windows background,

82
00:05:05,912 --> 00:05:10,150
is why with Windows, I just maybe find
some application that I want to use on

83
00:05:10,150 --> 00:05:14,393
the web and I download the executable and
I run the executable installer and

84
00:05:14,393 --> 00:05:17,530
I install it from whatever
website I downloaded from.

85
00:05:17,530 --> 00:05:20,285
So why doesn't Linux just do it that way,

86
00:05:20,285 --> 00:05:26,060
why would they use these packages that are
associated with the distribution instead?

87
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:30,802
And really, you can think of packages
as kind of a precursor to app stores

88
00:05:30,802 --> 00:05:32,279
that you have today for

89
00:05:32,279 --> 00:05:37,300
mobile phones that have become popular
with the iPhone and android phones.

90
00:05:37,300 --> 00:05:41,397
So the benefit of the package manager is
that it's one centralized location that's

91
00:05:41,397 --> 00:05:43,090
controlled by the distribution.

92
00:05:43,090 --> 00:05:46,591
That gives you a list of
trusted software sources.

93
00:05:46,591 --> 00:05:50,581
So rather than trying to download
from a bunch of different websites

94
00:05:50,581 --> 00:05:55,551
that are hosted by completely different
applications, you've got one place where

95
00:05:55,551 --> 00:06:00,101
someone in the community is helping to
maintain a list of all the packages that

96
00:06:00,101 --> 00:06:04,740
are configured to build and run and
install successfully in a distribution.

97
00:06:04,740 --> 00:06:07,919
So this helps solve some of
the fragmentation problems because every

98
00:06:07,919 --> 00:06:11,755
distribution is a little bit different in
terms of configuration that ships with,

99
00:06:11,755 --> 00:06:14,879
there might need to be slight
customization to software packages in

100
00:06:14,879 --> 00:06:17,440
order to make them run
properly on the distributions.

101
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:20,659
So by having a package manager
that's built into the distribution,

102
00:06:20,659 --> 00:06:23,932
you can solve some of those problems
because the distribution package

103
00:06:23,932 --> 00:06:27,096
manager will know which distribution
is getting installed into and

104
00:06:27,096 --> 00:06:29,979
it can put all the required
components in the correct paths and

105
00:06:29,979 --> 00:06:32,400
make sure that it runs
correctly on distribution.

106
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:37,011
So I mentioned before Ubuntu is
one of the popular distributions.

107
00:06:37,011 --> 00:06:39,310
It's the one that we're
going to use in this course.

108
00:06:39,310 --> 00:06:41,620
You can use other
distributions if you want.

109
00:06:41,620 --> 00:06:45,694
This is just the one that will
provide support in terms of myself and

110
00:06:45,694 --> 00:06:50,789
student assistance and this is one of the
things that I have pronounced wrong before

111
00:06:50,789 --> 00:06:56,029
I started teaching this course and had to
look up pronunciations, but it's actually

112
00:06:56,029 --> 00:07:01,100
pronounced uu-boonto and it's an African
word which means humanity to others.

113
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:03,542
The first release was back in 2004 and

114
00:07:03,542 --> 00:07:07,251
it's based on another distribution
that's known as Debian.

115
00:07:07,251 --> 00:07:09,410
Also popular distribution.

116
00:07:09,410 --> 00:07:14,756
And the kind of the motivation behind
Ubuntu over Debian is that it might

117
00:07:14,756 --> 00:07:19,833
be slightly less stable, but
it's also going to be more up to date,

118
00:07:19,833 --> 00:07:25,840
have more recent software packages
available than what you'd find in Debian.

119
00:07:25,840 --> 00:07:29,324
It's also more focused on
usability than licensing and

120
00:07:29,324 --> 00:07:34,460
what that means is that in Debian, there's
kind of an emphasis on free software.

121
00:07:34,460 --> 00:07:38,086
Again, free as I mentioned before,
as in liberty, like your freedom to do

122
00:07:38,086 --> 00:07:41,510
whatever you want with the software
to have access to the source code.

123
00:07:41,510 --> 00:07:45,626
Ubuntu is less focused on
making sure that for instance,

124
00:07:45,626 --> 00:07:50,001
every single Kodak that you'd install for
access to video or

125
00:07:50,001 --> 00:07:54,975
audio has a free and open license and
it's more focused on making sure

126
00:07:54,975 --> 00:07:59,371
that the system is as usable
as possible from installation.

127
00:07:59,371 --> 00:08:03,271
In other words, making it easy for users
who might be just getting started out.

128
00:08:03,271 --> 00:08:06,060
Like might be your case for this course.

129
00:08:06,060 --> 00:08:11,463
So the package management for Ubuntu is
traditionally performed by the apt utility

130
00:08:11,463 --> 00:08:16,350
that's still available and and
still a way that you can install packages.

131
00:08:16,350 --> 00:08:19,561
There's also recent support for
the snap utility and

132
00:08:19,561 --> 00:08:23,150
one nice thing about Ubuntu
is package management system.

133
00:08:23,150 --> 00:08:27,220
If you try to use an application that's
not currently installed, you'll get

134
00:08:27,220 --> 00:08:31,560
this message here that tells you how you
can install that particular application.

135
00:08:31,560 --> 00:08:35,256
So in this case, I'm trying to use
an application called tree to look

136
00:08:35,256 --> 00:08:37,830
at the details of a specific
folder structure.

137
00:08:37,830 --> 00:08:40,847
And it's giving me a helpful message
saying it can't be installed but

138
00:08:40,847 --> 00:08:42,450
here are some options to install it.

139
00:08:42,450 --> 00:08:46,703
So by simply doing sudo apt install tree,
I can have the tree utility that I was

140
00:08:46,703 --> 00:08:50,251
trying to use installed and
available in the operating system.

141
00:08:51,940 --> 00:08:56,224
So getting into a little bit more about
how we would install applications,

142
00:08:56,224 --> 00:09:00,644
you can imagine that it would make sense
that a default user account you create

143
00:09:00,644 --> 00:09:02,910
would have access limitations there.

144
00:09:02,910 --> 00:09:04,099
This would be important,

145
00:09:04,099 --> 00:09:07,250
especially if there's multiple
user accounts created on a system.

146
00:09:07,250 --> 00:09:11,114
But it's also helpful because it prevents
you from accidentally doing things you

147
00:09:11,114 --> 00:09:12,080
didn't want to do.

148
00:09:12,080 --> 00:09:15,738
So for instance,
if you would happen to use rm-rf and

149
00:09:15,738 --> 00:09:19,901
we'll talk a little bit more
about what the rm command does.

150
00:09:19,901 --> 00:09:24,247
But it's basically, this command is
going to be removing a directory or

151
00:09:24,247 --> 00:09:25,611
a set of directories.

152
00:09:25,611 --> 00:09:28,275
And if you happen to use
rm-rf with a slash and

153
00:09:28,275 --> 00:09:30,951
then referencing some undefined variable.

154
00:09:30,951 --> 00:09:35,116
Because the variable is undefined,
this would look like rm-rf/ and

155
00:09:35,116 --> 00:09:38,646
basically just start deleting
everything in your system,

156
00:09:38,646 --> 00:09:41,280
which is probably not
what you wanted to do.

157
00:09:41,280 --> 00:09:45,822
And so if you're running this rm-rf/
command as a normal user account,

158
00:09:45,822 --> 00:09:50,294
you get errors back saying that you
don't have the permissions to delete

159
00:09:50,294 --> 00:09:55,540
the contents of the file system, which in
this case would be a really good thing.

160
00:09:55,540 --> 00:10:00,400
But in some cases you
actually do need Root access.

161
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:04,487
And there are specific commands where you
need to have the permissions to access

162
00:10:04,487 --> 00:10:08,220
things in the root file system that
aren't allocated to specific user.

163
00:10:08,220 --> 00:10:11,590
So for that you would use what's
called the Root User account.

164
00:10:11,590 --> 00:10:18,340
And that account basically has access to
modify or change anything on the system.

165
00:10:18,340 --> 00:10:22,123
So the different levels that
are available to root user account or

166
00:10:22,123 --> 00:10:26,664
single users let the system administrator
configure who should have access to

167
00:10:26,664 --> 00:10:31,510
specific devices on the system, who should
have access to specific directories.

168
00:10:31,510 --> 00:10:34,255
And then again,
if you can either log in as a root user or

169
00:10:34,255 --> 00:10:38,040
impersonate the root user, you can
do whatever you want on the system.

170
00:10:38,040 --> 00:10:42,352
So Ubuntu each distribution
might have a slightly different

171
00:10:42,352 --> 00:10:45,151
philosophy on how it uses root access.

172
00:10:45,151 --> 00:10:50,520
Ubuntu's root access philosophy is
that when you configure your default

173
00:10:50,520 --> 00:10:55,390
user account, you do not have root
permissions for that account.

174
00:10:55,390 --> 00:10:58,779
However, if you are doing something
that you need permissions for,

175
00:10:58,779 --> 00:11:01,992
you can just use the sudo command
which is short for super user do,

176
00:11:01,992 --> 00:11:04,410
before whatever command
you're trying to run.

177
00:11:04,410 --> 00:11:07,616
So if you type sudo and then
the command that you're trying to run,

178
00:11:07,616 --> 00:11:09,680
it's going to ask you for
your password and

179
00:11:09,680 --> 00:11:13,577
if you enter your password successfully,
it's going to run the command that you

180
00:11:13,577 --> 00:11:17,220
specified as if you were a root user
rather than your current user account.

181
00:11:17,220 --> 00:11:21,275
So this is kind of a nice trade off in
that it prevents us from accidentally

182
00:11:21,275 --> 00:11:25,595
doing things we maybe didn't want to do
when we're running things as our user

183
00:11:25,595 --> 00:11:29,915
account, but it also gives us the option
to have root access when we need it for

184
00:11:29,915 --> 00:11:31,261
specific operations.